WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:20.619
STUDENT: How is the British context different for being in public life and being a person of faith? Because I know that when people

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.010
that you were doing-- taking this project on after leaving office, there was surprise that all of a sudden 

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.820
Tony Blair's a man of faith. 

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.490
But you said,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.940
"Originally, oh I've always been a man of faith," but 

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:39.660
there was always some element, that maybe you chose your 

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.070
timing wisely. Just curious about what that experience was like.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.980
BLAIR: Well, first of all, 

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.170
I think next seminar we'll have the wife [laughs]. 

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.120
She seems a saintly person.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.200
I...

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.820
I meanI mean, it was difficult-- actually what Tony said chimed

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.580
with me.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.680
there is a sense in which people could use God in a political debate

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.759
that really,

00:01:12.759 --> 00:01:16.760
if you actually are someone of faith, really makes you uncomfortable. 

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.780
And also, the British are a bit reserved about these things, frankly. So

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.340
-- the moment at the end of one speech-- I wanted to end the speech 

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.190
by saying,

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.619
"God bless Britain,"

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:31.970
you know? And I tell you, that was

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.540
"You can't possibly say that, Prime Minister," 

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.520
said a senior civil servant. I said, "Well, why not?"

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.470
One of them said, "Oh, for goodness' sake, Prime Minister, this isn't America, you know." 

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.230
So

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:48.700
there's always-- I think a--

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.630
a natural reserve that there is about it. 

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.470
But I actually-- I didn't hide the fact. I used to go to church every week and was often photographed there. 

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.790
But I actually had a reluctance

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:00.860
to talk about 

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.720
God, but-- I mean,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.460
I think, for me, 

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.090
the global poverty issue particularly was very linked with my religious-- 

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.499
religious belief and faith. 

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:17.249
But it was difficult, 'cause the trouble is-- 

00:02:17.249 --> 00:02:18.290
you know, the

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.899
problem is, in politics-- this is true, incidentally, in the debate 

00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:27.219
about globalization.

00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:28.989
And it's 

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:31.479
where sometimes there is an impatience

00:02:31.479 --> 00:02:34.579
from political people with religious people, 

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:37.939
because actually the decisions can be difficult, you know? 

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:42.289
And sometimes religious people can be on the side of...

00:02:42.289 --> 00:02:43.949
"If you only 

00:02:43.949 --> 00:02:45.749
cared more, then 

00:02:45.749 --> 00:02:49.819
it would be a lot easier." You know, if you're a political leader, and you're in this situation taking

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:50.870
decisions, you're saying, 

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.319
 "Yeah, I care, but I mean--

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:54.940
it's this way or that way, so which way do I go?" 

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.330
And you know, it can be very-- 

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.389
so that, I think, is part of the discourse

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:02.799
that's important. And I think one of the things that I think 

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:05.319
actually--

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:06.319
faith can

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:07.879
challenge political leadership,

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:12.389
but also an engagement between faith and political leadership also allows that to be a bit of 

00:03:12.389 --> 00:03:14.459
a two-way process.

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:18.299
So far example, in globalization, I've found the hardest thing, actually, 

00:03:18.299 --> 00:03:21.319
with the church groups on

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:26.069
the debt cancellation and the G8 agenda in 2005, which is a broad agenda, 

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:28.940
not just about aid and debt but also about 

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:29.749
governance-- 

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:33.489
you know, I would say to them, "I'm sorry, we're putting governance on the table, 'cause some of 

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.869
these countries are corrupt and that's their problem.

00:03:35.869 --> 00:03:38.519
It's not 'cause I'm not giving 'em enough money; 

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:42.899
the money that is being given ends up someplace in Switzerland. So that's not right."

00:03:42.899 --> 00:03:44.829
Or alternatively, 

00:03:44.829 --> 00:03:47.519
and more difficult in a way, 

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:50.349
you would have a fierce debate about free trade. 

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:54.049
Because there were elements within the global poverty movement that said," Free trade

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:56.549
is a snare and delusion for the poor, it

00:03:56.549 --> 00:03:58.489
actually increases poverty." 

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:01.500
And I would say, "Well, hang on a minute. That is a technical 

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.619
and political and economic question and we've gotta debate that. 

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:06.429
I'm not prepared just 

00:04:06.429 --> 00:04:08.079
to kind of accept that

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:11.619
just because you're a religious person you can say that to me and I've got to accept that as the morally 

00:04:11.619 --> 00:04:12.759
right position."

00:04:12.759 --> 00:04:13.719
So I think,

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.370
you know, there's that as well,

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:17.249
that interacts with all this. But I 

00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:20.259
agree with Tony in the sense that 

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:21.910
I think it would be 

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.069
really tragic

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:26.020
if the faith part got squeezed out of public life. 

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.080
In the sense that people actually felt embarrassed about 

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.199
their faith.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:32.779
And also, it would end up actually being 

00:04:32.779 --> 00:04:34.669
quite dishonest, because in fact, 

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:38.439
there are a lot of people in the world of politics, and amazing numbers of them, actually, 

00:04:38.439 --> 00:04:41.020
in senior political positions who do believe very strongly.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.959
Doesn't mean it always makes them better political leaders, 

00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:48.819
but it's important about them as human beings. 
STUDENT: And I think it is those who use faith in a sort of pandering way 

00:04:48.819 --> 00:04:51.689
are the ones that are sort of making it divisive

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:56.930
and make non-religious people sort of impatient, as you say, with religion.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.520
I think that this issue is actually something that we should 

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.889
talk about more broadly 

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:02.829
in this course. I mean, it's really important for getting the sort of 

00:05:02.829 --> 00:05:09.829
non-religious people in bed with those of faith. Environmental groups that are on the left, 

00:05:09.829 --> 00:05:13.400
religious groups working with Rick Warren, people like that-- 

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.069
you know, how do you get there, more broadly, is a really interesting question and an important question. 

00:05:15.069 --> 00:05:15.319
well

